<!--
  AP reassembly buffer-pool config — the SSOT for the tokio (AP / Linux)
  host's reassembly slot-pool dimensions and runtime knobs
  (reassembly-fsm prose-sketch §4 "Timer / quota / pool config").

  R311in — this file replaces the hand-transcribed `4 / 4096 / 2 / 500`
  constants formerly buried in `wz-runtime-tokio/session_glue.rs`. SCE
  is the typed SSOT owner of the `sce:kind="buffer-pool"` schema: its
  `sce-codegen` emits the spec-anchored `SLOT_COUNT` / `SLOT_SIZE` /
  `MAX_FRAGMENTS_PER_MESSAGE` / `REASSEMBLY_TIMEOUT_MS` / `PER_PEER_QUOTA`
  constants (plus the `ReassemblySlot` per-slot struct + size drift
  guards) that the host `ReassemblyDispatcher` consumes. The template
  comment is explicit that these constants exist "so the algorithm
  doesn't have to round-trip through the deploy.yaml" — the wz
  dispatcher is that intended downstream consumer. Authoring the values
  here once, and codegen'ing them, removes the prior triple-drift
  (spec §4 table / deploy.yaml inline block / session_glue consts).

  Values mirror `deploy/ap_mcu_pair.yaml` `ap_node.buffer_pools.
  reassembly_pool` (the AP machine of the canonical asymmetric deploy).
  The MCU machine has its own, smaller pool — see
  `reassembly_pool_mcu.scxml`. SCE's cross-doc deploy validators
  (`slot_size >= max_fragments * mtu`, `peer_table.capacity *
  per_peer_quota >= slot_count`) hold for these values against the AP
  link mtu 1472 / peer_table 256: 1048576 >= 44*1472 = 64768, and
  256*8 = 2048 >= 32.

  slot-size was 65536 until it was raised to 1 MiB. The old value was
  derived from the 1472-byte AP LINK mtu of this deploy (44*1472 =
  64768, rounded up to 65536) — correct for that link, but it was then
  the per-chain reassembly CAP for EVERY AP link, and a TCP unicast link
  negotiates a 65535-byte batch. A chain only forms when the frame
  exceeds the batch, so on TCP the reassembly window was
  (65535, 65536] — one byte wide, which made `transport-fragmentation`
  unobservable end-to-end on the AP profile.

  slot-size is no longer a bound on a message. The 1 MiB cap it became
  was still below upstream's: the fan-in lane measured 4,000,000-byte
  samples arriving 0% wherever wz was on either end, because the AP
  Router type was built with slot-size as its per-chain cap and the
  sender refused by the same value. An AP chain is now bounded only by
  the session's configured transport/link/rx/max_message_size
  (upstream's 1 GiB by default), as upstream's defragmentation buffer is
  (wz-runtime-tokio's reassembly module, REASSEMBLY_CHAIN_CAP).
  max-fragments-per-message is likewise not enforced by the AP Router.

  What slot-size is, per arena: the default AP arena (`alloc`,
  HeapStaging) does not read it at all and stages each chain on the
  heap, so an idle chain costs nothing. The reserved arena
  (`runtime-zero-copy`, PooledStaging) allocates the 32 x 1 MiB table
  once and stages a chain in a slot while it fits one, so a message of
  up to 1 MiB costs no allocation; a longer chain moves to a heap
  buffer as it outgrows its slot and sends the slot home. slot-count is
  how many chains either arena stages at once.

  The default arena consumes the reassembly constants only. The
  buffer-pool template's §5.E slot-lifecycle API (Slot<S> / pool struct)
  is consumed by the reserved arena (wz-runtime-tokio's zero_copy
  module: pool_acquire_for_encode / write / pool_return), and compiles
  as dead code in a build without it.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="buffer-pool" name="reassembly_pool_ap" version="1.0">
  <sce:variant>reassembly</sce:variant>
  <sce:slot-count>32</sce:slot-count>
  <sce:slot-size>1048576</sce:slot-size>
  <sce:section>heap</sce:section>
  <sce:alignment>64</sce:alignment>
  <sce:cache-policy>none</sce:cache-policy>
  <sce:max-fragments-per-message>44</sce:max-fragments-per-message>
  <sce:reassembly-timeout-ms>500</sce:reassembly-timeout-ms>
  <sce:per-peer-quota>8</sce:per-peer-quota>
</scxml>
